{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DeMjo8a8brgspDK8pmK6n7","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! I've found clear patterns related to SIGPIPE and write error handling. Here's my report:\n\n## SIGPIPE and Write Error Testing Patterns Found\n\nI examined 4 cmd/ test files and found explicit SIGPIPE handling tests in 3 of them:\n\n### 1. **cmd/yes/yes_test.go** (Lines 51-75)\n- Function: `TestExitCodeOnSIGPIPE()`\n- Pattern: Creates a pipe to stdout, reads 1 line, then **closes the pipe** while the program is still running\n- Simulates broken pipe scenario by closing stdout\n- Expects exit code 0 and no stderr output\n- Tests both default behavior and with arguments\n\n### 2. **cmd/du/du_test.go** (Lines 76-102)\n- Function: `TestSIGPIPE()`\n- Pattern: Creates a large fixture (50 directories, 5 files each), pipes output, reads only 1 byte, then **closes stdout**\n- Includes timeout check to verify the program doesn't hang waiting to write\n- Uses `context.WithTimeout()` to detect if SIGPIPE handler is missing (timeout = handler issue)\n- Expects exit code 0\n\n### 3. **cmd/cat/cat_test.go** (Lines 83-112)\n- Function: `TestSIGPIPE()`\n- Pattern: Creates large file (500,000 lines), pipes output, reads 1 byte, then **closes stdout**\n- Same timeout-based approach to detect handler installation\n- Includes comment: \"R5.4: cat must exit 0 when stdout is closed by a downstream consumer\"\n- Timeout indicates SIGPIPE handler may not be installed\n\n### 4. **cmd/head/head_test.go** (Lines 16-151)\n- No explicit SIGPIPE test found\n- Contains comprehensive diff tests and error handling tests but no broken pipe simulation\n\n## Key Patterns Across Tests:\n1. All SIGPIPE tests use the same approach: read minimal data, close pipe, check exit code\n2. They use `context.WithTimeout()` as a canary to detect handler installation failures\n3. Programs are expected to exit gracefully with code 0 when stdout closes\n4. The pattern matches real-world pipe behavior like `cmd | head -1`"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"25451b85-6808-4478-950a-0e4ef670951c","uuid":"80595751-0c72-4cfb-919e-e2ffc197a4f3","timestamp":"2026-05-15T03:14:03.150Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"In /private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5253, search cmd/ test files for patterns related to testing SIGPIPE handling or write errors on stdout. Look for things like \"SIGPIPE\", \"sigpipe\", \"write error\", \"broken pipe\", \"head -1\", or any test that pipes output through a limited reader. Check 3-4 different cmd/*_test.go files. Report what patterns you find, if any. Quick search.","agentId":"a350eae92728f87d1","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! I've found clear patterns related to SIGPIPE and write error handling. Here's my report:\n\n## SIGPIPE and Write Error Testing Patterns Found\n\nI examined 4 cmd/ test files and found explicit SIGPIPE handling tests in 3 of them:\n\n### 1. **cmd/yes/yes_test.go** (Lines 51-75)\n- Function: `TestExitCodeOnSIGPIPE()`\n- Pattern: Creates a pipe to stdout, reads 1 line, then **closes the pipe** while the program is still running\n- Simulates broken pipe scenario by closing stdout\n- Expects exit code 0 and no stderr output\n- Tests both default behavior and with arguments\n\n### 2. **cmd/du/du_test.go** (Lines 76-102)\n- Function: `TestSIGPIPE()`\n- Pattern: Creates a large fixture (50 directories, 5 files each), pipes output, reads only 1 byte, then **closes stdout**\n- Includes timeout check to verify the program doesn't hang waiting to write\n- Uses `context.WithTimeout()` to detect if SIGPIPE handler is missing (timeout = handler issue)\n- Expects exit code 0\n\n### 3. **cmd/cat/cat_test.go** (Lines 83-112)\n- Function: `TestSIGPIPE()`\n- Pattern: Creates large file (500,000 lines), pipes output, reads 1 byte, then **closes stdout**\n- Same timeout-based approach to de